<pre><code># let twist f = reverse_curry2 f;;
val twist : ('a -&gt; 'b -&gt; 'c) -&gt; 'b -&gt; 'a -&gt; 'c = &lt;fun&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: NSubstitute for Protected Methods A: <p>If you want to test a <code>DelegatingHandler</code> or <code>HttpMessageHandler</code>, you do not have to worry about mocking the protected <code>SendAsync()</code> method. I've used a combination of test <code>DelegatingHandler</code>s and <code>HttpMessageInvoker</code> (note the <code>HttpMessageInvokerFactory</code>) in below answer.</p>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/31650718/unit-testing-delegatinghandler/74608985#74608985">Unit testing DelegatingHandler</a></p>
<p>Along similar lines of above, you can just create a handler just for testing with a lambda (or an interface) as</p>
<pre><code>/// &lt;summary&gt;
/// Handler for testing.
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
public class TestHandler : DelegatingHandler
{
    private readonly Func&lt;HttpRequestMessage, CancellationToken, Task&lt;HttpResponseMessage&gt;&gt; func;

    /// &lt;inheritdoc cref=&quot;TestHandler&quot; /&gt;
    public TestHandler(Func&lt;HttpRequestMessage, CancellationToken, Task&lt;HttpResponseMessage&gt;&gt; func)
    {
        this.func = func;
    }

    protected override async Task&lt;HttpResponseMessage&gt; SendAsync(
        HttpRequestMessage request,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        return await func(request, cancellationToken);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>and</p>
<pre><code>[Test]
public async Task Verify_Something()
{
    var fixture = new Fixture().Customize(new AutoNSubstituteCustomization());
    var testHandler = new TestHandler(async (request, ct) =&gt;
    {
        // some test setup logic
        // return http 200
        return new HttpResponseMessage(HttpStatusCode.OK);
    });
    using var invoker = HttpMessageInvokerFactory.Create(testHandler);
    using var request = fixture.Create&lt;HttpRequestMessage&gt;();
    using var response = await invoker.SendAsync(request, CancellationToken.None);
    // verify here
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can use something similar to above for <code>HttpMessageHandler</code>.</p>
